    "result_text": "Build is green. The full suite (`AcceptanceTest`, the only test class) passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephones in `OwnerTelephone.normalize` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerTelephone.java`).\n\n**The rule:** after stripping formatting and resolving the country code, the national number (digits after the country code) must have the exact length the country requires \u2014 `+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10. A wrong length makes `normalize` return `null`, which `ValidateNewOwner` turns into an `InvalidTelephoneException`, handled as a **400** with `{\"errors\":[\"telephone\"]}`.\n\nDetails:\n- Added a `hasValidNationalLength` helper that prefix-matches the country code (`61` before `1`, so the two don't collide) and checks the remaining digit count. Unrecognised country codes fall back to the existing generic 8\u201315 digit check.\n- Updated the Javadoc to document the new constraint.\n\nThis is a strict superset of the prior behavior: the test's `+61 123` was already rejected by the min-length check, but now a `+61` number with 8 or 10 national digits (within the old 8\u201315 total range) is also correctly rejected, fully satisfying the spec. Existing valid data stays valid \u2014 `ownerNode()`'s `04XXXXXXXX` normalizes to `+61` with 9 national digits, and the `+1` seed data carries 10.",
